# Repository structure

- Apply this rule when creating, moving or renaming files, or changing imports, tooling and build paths.
- The repository holds two apps. Backend work happens in `apps/api/`; create the Next.js application under `apps/web/` when it begins. Do not scaffold empty target directories.
- `apps/web/`: Next.js application and its package manifest, one JavaScript lockfile, framework configuration, `public/` and frontend tests.
- `apps/web/src/app/`: App Router routes, layouts, loading/error boundaries and route composition; keep domain behavior in feature modules.
- `apps/web/src/features/<feature>/`: feature-owned components, hooks, types and tests; start with the features the implementation actually needs.
- `apps/web/src/components/ui/`: UI primitives shared by real consumers. `src/lib/`: focused infrastructure such as API transport and server-side session handling. Keep server-only code outside client imports.
- `apps/api/`: Python project manifest, its `uv.lock`, Alembic configuration, migrations, backend tests (package `tests`), API-only `scripts/` and the app `Dockerfile`. Run its tools with `uv run --project apps/api`; the image builds from the repository root context.
- `apps/api/src/sentinel/`: installed FastAPI package; retain the `sentinel` import name and existing focused modules. The static UI lives in the package until the web app replaces it. The wheel force-includes `migrations/` and `alembic.ini` as `sentinel/_alembic/`; edit only the copies in `apps/api/`.
- `tests/e2e/`: flows spanning frontend and backend (package `e2e`, own `pytest.ini`, run in the API environment). `tests/fixtures/`: shared synthetic evidence and locked evaluation manifests. App-local tests belong with their app; keep each fixture authoritative in one place. Run the API and e2e suites as separate pytest sessions; they must not share a package name.
- `scripts/`: repository-wide development and maintenance commands. Put scripts serving only one app under that app's `scripts/`.
- `infra/`: deployment configuration shared across apps, when needed. Root `compose.yaml` may remain the local orchestration entry point; app Dockerfiles belong with their app.
- `prompts/`: reusable prompts. `docs/`: the existing canonical documentation and change records. Retain engine-required document paths and root materialized guides.
- `data/`: ignored private runtime state, databases, encryption/recovery keys, sources, backups and local tools. Relative `data/...` settings resolve against `WORKSPACE_ROOT` (default: the repository root), never the working directory. Public frontend assets must never contain these objects.
- `output/`, `test-results/`, caches and build products are generated artifacts. Keep them out of application source; preserve existing untracked user material.
- Keep root files for repository entry points, licenses, cross-app orchestration/tooling and required materialized guides. Keep app-only manifests/configuration with the app.
- Use kebab-case for new TypeScript filenames, PascalCase for exported React components and snake_case for Python modules. Preserve Next.js special filenames, locked fixture names and canonical/generated document names.
- Keep frontend/backend boundaries at the API. Add a shared package only when existing consumers require it; keep feature-specific helpers with their feature.
- Prefer descriptive modules over catch-all `utils`, `common` or `misc` folders. Preserve existing module names unless a verified migration changes them.
- For moves, inventory tracked/untracked files and record old-to-new paths. Update imports, packaging, resources, tests, ignores, CI, startup commands, build contexts and documentation in the same reviewable slice.
- Preserve fixture bytes/hashes, Alembic revision IDs, historical records and private data paths. Never regenerate keys or change review rules as a side effect of folder cleanup.
- Verify package installation/imports outside the source tree, migrations, affected tests and available build checks before declaring a move complete. Report unavailable PostgreSQL/container gates as pending.
- Read `prompts/organize-repository.md` when executing a further reorganization. This rule governs placement; the prompt supplies the migration procedure. The first migration is recorded in `docs/changes/repository-organization/`.
